{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"trivial-protected-barracuda","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Replacement/residual bug split from witted-invisible-roan after completion verification refused close on 2026-09-07.\n\nResidual defect:\nThe configured rebar MCP endpoint can still return persistent HTTP 502 after concurrent gate load if the MCP process is OOM-killed or otherwise dies while nginx's materialized single-server upstream still points at the dead backend. The parent landed detection, retire-sweep repair, memory instrumentation, and a reduced whole-store read footprint, but those do not prove the serving path cannot recur under concurrent gate load.\n\nVerifier blocker on parent, summarized:\n- nginx still has a single materialized upstream with no failover path;\n- memory oversubscription under concurrent gates is not bounded by admission control, instance sizing, or off-box execution;\n- existing comments identify the latency/read-scan issue (`lousy-unsuited-gnu` / `94c5-b42e-ac80-4f06`) as separate and not fixed by the memory reduction.\n\nDesign question to resolve here:\nChoose the prevention mechanism(s) for recurrence — e.g. MCP upstream failover/self-healing, gate admission/concurrency limits, moving gate execution off-box, or a data-backed capacity change — and implement enough that `/mcp` cannot remain pinned to a dead backend after gate-induced MCP failure.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- [ ] The selected design is recorded with why alternatives were accepted/rejected.\n- [ ] A reproduced or simulated MCP backend death under gate-like load recovers without a persistent nginx 502.\n- [ ] The prevention is covered by tests through the relevant surface(s) and by an operator-visible metric/log/alarm.\n- [ ] Any capacity/concurrency number used by the prevention cites measured data, including or linking the AC5 campaign split to `inherited-americium-porpoise`.\n","id":"85a5-97ff-4a47-4c77","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Prevent persistent MCP 502 recurrence"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788753773815803001,"uuid":"6d463cf6-542d-4fbb-a497-77f6087d40a8"}